ns-child-frame-resize: show the new area when a chrome-less child frame grows.

NS (Cocoa) only.  Growing a child frame that has no window chrome
(undecorated, or undecorated-round) through one -[NSWindow setFrame:]
shows the added area black for one or more display frames: the commits
that land in the display frame of the shape change are presented clipped
to the old size at the new origin, until a commit in a later frame.
display:YES, a CATransaction flush, -displayIfNeeded, drawing before the
resize and changes to the layer's actions or contentsGravity do not help.
A titled window (decorated frames) is not affected.

ns_set_window_size and ns_set_window_size_and_position now grow such a
window in two steps.  change_frame_size runs first as before, so Lisp sees
the new size at once.  Step 1 sets the window frame to the union of its
current size and the new size, at its current origin, while the EmacsView
is held at its old frame: resizeWithOldSuperviewSize: puts the view back
and skips its bookkeeping, and [EmacsLayer display] draws nothing, so the
screen does not change.  Step 2, 20 ms later (a cancellable delayed
perform on the view), clears the hold, sets the final window frame, sizes
the view to its superview, does the bookkeeping of
resizeWithOldSuperviewSize: and redisplays, guarded as in
layoutSublayersOfLayer:, so the new surface is committed with the new
area.  A new resize cancels a pending step 2 and starts again from the
current window frame; deleting the frame cancels it.  Shrinks, moves,
top-level frames and decorated frames use the single -setFrame: as before.
constrainFrameRect:toScreen: already returns its argument for child
frames, so it does not alter the step 1 rectangle.

The extension of step 1 must not be visible, so the window of a child
frame is non-opaque with a clear background.  ns_update_window_opacity
sets that and, when a frame gains or loses its parent frame, sets it or
restores the default face background; ns_set_background_color leaves the
window of a child frame alone.  Windows of top-level frames, undecorated
ones included, keep their opacity and background.  Touches only
src/nsterm.m, src/nsterm.h and src/nsfns.m.

